Triumphantly, she carried the first star downward. But as it descended through clouds, through wind, through thickening air, something changed. The star that had blazed eternally began to flicker. By the time she reached the ground, she held only a dying ember.

"You've broken it!" the other creatures cried.

"No," said the ancient owl, arriving. "She revealed its nature. Stars burn bright in the heavens because they're shared by all eyes, needed by all travelers, belonging to no one. When she tried to make it hers alone, it became merely a thing again—and things without purpose fade."

The magpie watched the ember crumble to dust. Above, the gap in the constellation seemed like a missing tooth in a smile.

She understood finally: her ancestors hadn't lost the flowers by giving them away. They'd transformed them into something that couldn't be owned—which meant they could never be lost.

