Myth: After the hermit crab's hoarded light died, he watched the tide pools more carefully.

He saw baby clams filtering moonlight-fed algae into pearls. He saw starfish passing nutrients through hundreds of tube feet into coral colonies. He saw how each creature took something in, transformed it, and released something new—none holding tight, all participating in an endless exchange.

"I want to join this chain," he told the turtle. "But I'm small. What can I transform?"

The turtle smiled. "You already do. Watch your shell."

The crab looked closer. Tiny barnacles had attached to his shell, filtering water as he wandered. Their waste fed amphipods. The amphipods attracted fish. The fish brought nutrients that nourished new algae blooms.

"I thought chains required intention," the crab whispered.

"The chain simply requires movement," the turtle replied. "Hoarding stops flow. But when you move through the world, taking what you need and releasing what you don't, you become a living vessel—transformation happens through you, whether you notice or not."

The crab understood then: participating doesn't mean being extraordinary. It means simply staying in motion, letting gifts pass through rather than stopping within.

That night, moonlight touched his shell, and he kept walking.

